    "id": "b1b2db71-44f6-4d1e-8ff5-7ad07f4b3d02",
    "category": "Football",
    "title": "Harry Kane’s Patience and the Tottenham Years That Led to the Ballon d’Or",
    "excerpt": "Tottenham gave Harry Kane a platform, but not the trophy-winning team his talent seemed to deserve. After a record-breaking Bayern season and the 2026 Ballon d’Or, the old debate has changed.",
    "body": "For years, Tottenham gave Harry Kane a platform big enough to reveal his greatness and a ceiling that seemed to keep it just out of reach. He scored, created, pressed, dropped into midfield and carried the responsibility of a superstar, yet the conversation around him was repeatedly interrupted by the same question about what he had actually won. The question was fair as a measure of team achievement. It was never a fair measure of Kane’s ability.\n\nKane did not emerge as a finished legend. He had to fight for his place, go out on loan and convince a succession of managers that the local academy striker could lead a team. Once he did, he became the centre of Tottenham’s attack for almost a decade. His game was larger than the traditional image of a number nine. He could finish first time, hold off a defender, bend a pass into a runner’s path and retreat into midfield to begin an attack. A striker who does all that can make a team better even when he is not the one taking the final shot.\n\nThe numbers tell part of the story. Kane left Tottenham as its all-time leading scorer and with 213 Premier League goals for the club, the league record for goals at one team. He won three Premier League Golden Boots and once finished as the division’s leading playmaker. His partnership with Son Heung-min produced 47 Premier League goal combinations, a record for one player assisting another. Those are not the statistics of a player hiding in a comfortable team. They are the record of someone repeatedly making himself the solution.\n\nYet Tottenham’s era with Kane ended without the major trophy that would have changed how the story was told. The club reached the 2019 Champions League final and finished second in the league twice during his time there, but it did not convert those near-misses into a title. That is where the feeling of failure comes from. Kane was not owed a trophy by the universe. Football is never a solo sport. When a club has a player of his calibre through his prime, it has a responsibility to build a side capable of turning exceptional seasons into something lasting. Spurs came close, then fell short.\n\nIt is too simple to say Tottenham alone held him back. Kane stayed because he loved the club, believed in its project and wanted to be the player who changed its story. That loyalty became part of his public image and, at times, a trap. Every season without a trophy added another layer to the debate, while the same striker kept producing. His patience gave Tottenham years of elite goalscoring. It also made it easy for critics to treat the absence of medals as if it were a flaw in the man rather than a collective failure.\n\nLeaving for Bayern Munich in 2023 was not a retreat from that story. It was a test of what Kane could do when placed in a team built to dominate domestic matches and compete deep in Europe. The pressure was immediate. Bayern had won the Bundesliga for eleven straight seasons before Kane arrived. The club expected another title. Kane scored at a remarkable rate, but Bayern’s first season ended without a major trophy. The familiar joke followed him across borders. Was Kane the problem? Or was the story refusing to give him a winner’s ending?\n\nThat question changed in the seasons that followed. Kane helped Bayern win the Bundesliga in 2024 to 2025, then produced a historic 2025 to 2026 campaign. He scored 73 goals for club and country, won three trophies with Bayern and finished as the Bundesliga’s top scorer for the third season running. On 26 October 2026, he was awarded the men’s Ballon d’Or, becoming the first Englishman to win it in 25 years. The award did not erase the years in north London. It clarified the story of those years. Kane was a world-class player inside a team that could not always match his level.\n\nThe Ballon d’Or is not a mathematical proof that one footballer is better than every other player in every era. It is a vote and a snapshot of a season. But Kane’s 2026 win makes a once-provocative claim impossible to dismiss. He belongs in the conversation with the defining forwards of his generation. Comparisons with Lionel Messi, Cristiano Ronaldo and Robert Lewandowski will always be shaped by different roles, teams and careers. Kane does not need to be declared superior to them to be considered among the best. His case is that he combined elite finishing with the passing range and tactical intelligence of a creator. He sustained that level across leagues and international football.\n\nFor England, Kane has also carried the burden of being both captain and focal point. That can make a striker’s work look lonely. The goals are counted. Missed chances by teammates often are not. Every tournament ends with a judgement about whether the whole team went far enough. The 2026 Ballon d’Or arrived after a season in which his club output and international performances made the case together. He was not simply a penalty-box finisher collecting service. He was the reference point around which teams built attacks.\n\nThe more interesting question now is not whether Tottenham ruined Kane. That would erase the extraordinary player he became at Spurs and the affection he still holds there. It is whether Tottenham’s inability to win with him should change how we judge the club’s ambition. Kane gave the team a rare advantage. He could score at elite volume and help design the attack. The club reached the edge of the summit with him, then failed to take the last step. His departure made that missed opportunity more visible.\n\nHarry Kane waited, worked and kept producing while the debate asked for medals. He finally moved to a side equipped to win them, then delivered a season that carried him to football’s most famous individual prize. Tottenham helped introduce him to the world. Bayern gave him the stage to complete the argument. His greatness was never created by a Ballon d’Or. But the award made it harder for anyone to pretend that the trophyless years at Tottenham told the whole story.",
    "source_url": "https://www.premierleague.com/en/news/3628922\nhttps://www.premierleague.com/en/stats/records\nhttps://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/02aa-21ec47378e70-b29defb43769-1000--harry-kane-wins-2026-men-s-ballon-d-or/\nhttps://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/harry-kane-wins-2026-ballon-dor-bayern-munich-england-world-cup-39730\nhttps://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/harry-kane-2025-26-numbers-england-bauern-munich-goals-records-messi-38301",
