    "id": "2ef2a4fe-0b37-4603-afbd-00b5660b10cf",
    "category": "Entertainment",
    "title": "The Strange Objects Behind Movie Sounds",
    "excerpt": "That screen door, crunching footstep or punch may have been made with an object nowhere near the scene. Meet the sound artists who make imaginary worlds feel solid.",
    "body": "A hero walks across gravel. A door slams in a haunted house. Someone takes a bite of an apple. On screen, those sounds seem to belong to the scene. In the studio, they may come from a small collection of carefully chosen objects and a pair of creative hands.\n\nFoley artists perform everyday noises in time with a film. Shoes on different surfaces become footsteps. A folded jacket might rustle like a crowd. A handful of celery can produce a crunchy effect for a movie monster. The goal is not to trick an audience with a perfect imitation; it is to give a scene the physical detail our ears expect.\n\nSound also helps viewers understand where an action is happening and how shots connect. Research on film viewing found that dialogue and environmental sounds can help audiences link one shot to the next, although unfamiliar scenes can still be hard to interpret.\n\nNext time a film makes you jump, notice the layers: dialogue, music, room sound and those tiny performances hiding underneath. The most convincing movie sound can be a little bit of theatre.\n\nFoley is named for Jack Foley, an early film sound pioneer, but the craft is still wonderfully physical. The performer watches the picture and makes the sound at the exact moment it is needed. A pair of shoes, a tray of gravel, a creaking chair and a collection of fabrics can become a whole world when they are recorded with care. The movement has to match the actor’s timing, weight and pace; a footstep that lands a fraction too late can make a scene feel strangely unreal.\n\nSound artists also think about distance. A close, dry sound feels near; a softer, roomier one can suggest that the action is farther away. The sound may not be a literal recording of what appears on screen. It needs to tell the audience what the moment feels like. When you watch your next action scene, listen for the small sounds between the dialogue: cloth, breath, shoes and objects being handled. Those details are often the hidden glue that makes an imaginary place feel touchable.",
    "source_url": "https://www.acmi.net.au/education/school-program-and-resources/foley-sound/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29224446/",
