    "id": "606ea7c9-6abc-4928-9318-affda5cc14d7",
    "category": "Science",
    "title": "On Venus, a Day Lasts Longer Than a Year",
    "excerpt": "Venus spins so slowly that one full turn takes longer than one trip around the Sun. Its sunrise also arrives from the west.",
    "body": "Imagine celebrating New Year’s Day before your first Venusian sunrise is finished. On Venus, one rotation takes about 243 Earth days, while a trip around the Sun takes about 225 Earth days.\n\nThat means a Venus day, measured from one sunrise to the next, is a different length again: roughly 117 Earth days. The planet also rotates in the opposite direction to most planets in our solar system, so the Sun would rise in the west.\n\nThe planet’s slow, backward spin is one reason Venus is such an intriguing world to compare with Earth. It is similar to Earth in size, yet its thick atmosphere traps heat and its surface is hot enough to melt lead. “Earth’s twin” is a useful size comparison, not a promise of a welcoming neighbour.\n\nSpace facts can make familiar words feel less fixed. A day sounds simple until the planet under it decides to spin backwards.\n\nThe word “day” seems obvious until astronomers have to define it. A rotation day measures one complete spin relative to distant stars; a solar day measures the time from one noon to the next. Because Venus is moving around the Sun while it turns, those measurements differ. That is why the familiar headline that a Venus day is longer than its year needs a little unpacking: its slow rotation takes about 243 Earth days, its orbit about 225, and the sunrise-to-sunrise interval is shorter than either.\n\nVenus also rotates retrograde, in the opposite direction to most planets. If you could stand on its surface and survive the heat and pressure, the Sun would appear to rise in the west. The planet’s dense atmosphere makes the surface a hostile place, despite Venus being close to Earth in size. Keep those contrasts in mind when you hear “Earth’s twin.” It is a useful comparison for dimensions, not a description of the weather. A familiar planet can still run on a completely different clock.",
    "source_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/\nhttps://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/venus/overview/",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
    "date": "28 October 2026",
