    "id": "cd3383b6-373f-4e8c-bf7b-0d285c759869",
    "category": "Digital Skills",
    "title": "The Spreadsheet Skill That Saves More Time Than Fancy Formulas",
    "excerpt": "Clear labels, one row per record and a clean table make spreadsheets easier to sort, check and reuse.",
    "body": "A spreadsheet does not need an advanced formula to become useful. Often the biggest improvement is arranging the information so that you and the computer can both read it.\n\nGive each column a clear heading, keep one record per row and avoid mixing several types of information in the same cell. Turn the range into a table when your spreadsheet app supports it; then sorting, filtering and adding new rows becomes much easier.\n\nMicrosoft’s Excel guidance shows how tables can organise data and extend formulas as new rows are added. Once the structure is sound, simple functions such as SUM, AVERAGE and COUNT can answer practical questions.\n\nTry building a small tracker for stock, expenses or study time. Start with a handful of columns, add realistic sample rows, then test whether you can sort and filter it. Good structure is the quiet skill behind a spreadsheet that keeps working next month.\n\nA clean table turns a sheet from a pile of cells into a small, dependable data set. Imagine tracking event registrations: one row per person, one column each for name, contact, payment status and date. That shape makes it much easier to filter unpaid registrations, sort by date or count how many people are coming. If a cell contains several unrelated details, a spreadsheet cannot reliably sort or calculate them.\n\nBefore adding formulas, decide what each column means and keep the same kind of value in it. Use real date values rather than typing dates as free-form notes, and avoid blank rows inside the data. Then try one simple question: how many entries are complete, or what is the total amount received? A table can expand as you add records and keep formatting consistent. Good structure makes a spreadsheet easier for a colleague to understand, too. The clever formula is useful; the clear data underneath is what lets it work.",
    "source_url": "https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel\nhttps://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/create-and-format-tables-e81aa349-b006-4f8a-9806-5af9df0ac664",
