    "id": "095a990a-526c-4489-b2f2-ac4afc2725ed",
    "category": "Digital Guides",
    "title": "Passkeys Explained: Signing In Without a Traditional Password",
    "excerpt": "A passkey uses your device’s unlock method to prove it is you. Here is what happens and when to add one.",
    "body": "A passkey lets you sign in using a fingerprint, face scan or device screen lock instead of typing a password. It is tied to your account and device ecosystem, so there is no reusable secret for a fake login page to trick you into typing.\n\nGoogle explains that passkeys can provide stronger protection against phishing because they cannot be copied or accidentally handed to someone else in the way a password can. Your biometric data stays on your device; the service receives a confirmation, not your fingerprint.\n\nTo set one up, open your account’s security settings on a device you own and follow the service’s instructions. Keep your recovery options current, and do not create a passkey on a shared device that other people can unlock.\n\nA passkey can make everyday sign-in easier, but it does not remove the value of account recovery planning. Make sure you still know how to regain access if you lose your phone.\n\nA passkey uses public-key cryptography: your device keeps a private credential and proves possession of it when you sign in. The service stores a related public key. Because the passkey is associated with the legitimate website or app, it is designed to resist the trick where a fake page asks you to type a password. Your fingerprint or face scan unlocks the device locally; it is not sent to the website as your password.\n\nYou may be able to use a passkey across devices through a password manager or platform account, depending on the service and your settings. That convenience makes recovery planning important. Check which devices or accounts can restore access, keep your recovery email and phone current, and avoid creating a passkey on a shared device that another person can unlock. When a site offers passkeys, read the setup screen and consider adding one on a device you control. The aim is easier sign-in with fewer reusable secrets—not the belief that you can never be locked out.",
    "source_url": "https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/13548313?hl=en\nhttps://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
