    "id": "975cd69a-d9fa-4c8e-81b3-e77220c38d53",
    "category": "Digital Guides",
    "title": "A Two-Minute Check for Apps That Can See Too Much",
    "excerpt": "Your phone gives you control over permissions such as location, contacts, camera and microphone. Review them when an app no longer needs access.",
    "body": "An app may ask for access to your camera, contacts or location. Sometimes that permission is essential; sometimes it made sense for one task and never needed to stay on.\n\nOn Android, open Settings, choose Apps, select an app and tap Permissions. You can review access there or manage permissions by type. Google notes that the exact options vary by Android version and device.\n\nAsk what the app actually needs. A map app may need location while you are navigating. A photo editor may need access to selected images. A simple calculator usually has little reason to read your contacts. If a permission seems unnecessary, choose “Don’t allow” or the narrowest option that still lets the app work.\n\nA quick permission review is not a one-time security ritual. Repeat it when you install an unfamiliar app or when an app’s purpose changes. Your phone is yours; its permissions should match what you want it to do.\n\nPermissions are a way of giving an app access to parts of your device. A map needs location to show your position, but it may only need that access while you are using it. A messaging app may need your camera when you send a photo. The useful question is not “Is this permission always bad?” It is “Does this app need this access for the thing I am asking it to do?”\n\nOn Android, you can review permissions from an individual app’s settings or by permission type. Choose the narrowest option that still lets the feature work, such as selected photos or access only while in use when those choices are available. If you deny something and a feature stops working, you can revisit the setting later. Also check whether an app has access to location, microphone, camera, contacts and notifications that you no longer want to share. A quick review puts you back in control of the trade-off between convenience and access.",
    "source_url": "https://support.google.com/android/answer/9431959?hl=en",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
