    "id": "1eeefbbf-5462-4059-b552-c5c67c32d80b",
    "category": "Latest Information",
    "title": "Webb Peers Through a Stellar Nursery’s Dusty Curtain",
    "excerpt": "NASA’s Webb telescope has revealed young stars and energetic jets in NGC 7129, about 3,300 light-years away.",
    "body": "A new Webb image of NGC 7129 looks like a glowing cosmic landscape, with gold clouds, blue stars and red plumes. The colours help scientists distinguish different kinds of gas and activity; they are not what a human eye would necessarily see in space.\n\nThe region is a stellar nursery about 3,300 light-years from Earth. Webb’s infrared instruments can see through some of the dust that hides young stars from other kinds of telescopes. Its observations reveal protostars ejecting material and shaping the clouds around them.\n\nThose jets and outflows can carve cavities in the surrounding gas. They can also compress nearby material, contributing to conditions where new stars may form. The image is both beautiful and useful: it gives astronomers a closer look at how stars interact with their birthplace.\n\nLook at the red plume and the golden cavity and you are seeing a process that unfolds over millions of years, captured in a single telescope view.\n\nA stellar nursery is crowded with gas and dust, so ordinary visible-light images can hide what is happening inside. Infrared instruments can peer through some of that dusty material and reveal warmer regions, young stars and structures that were difficult to separate before. The Webb telescope’s sharp view lets astronomers study those details at a range of wavelengths, each of which highlights a different part of the scene.\n\nThe image is not just a prettier photograph. Scientists compare its patterns with other observations to ask where stars are forming, how radiation affects the surrounding cloud and what material remains available for new systems. A colourful composite translates measurements into a form people can inspect, but the palette is chosen for communication; it is not necessarily what a human observer would see with their eyes. When you open the original release, look for the instrument name, the wavelength and the caption’s explanation. Those details turn a striking image into a window on how astronomers build an answer.",
    "source_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/nasas-webb-captures-commotion-from-nebulas-stellar-jets/",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
    "date": "28 October 2026",
